{
 "help": "usage: wgsextract realign [-h] [--r1 R1] [--r2 R2]\n                          [--aligner {auto,bwa,bwa-mem2,bwa-mem3,minimap2,pbmm2}] [--fast]\n                          [--prefer-native-gpu | --no-prefer-native-gpu]\n                          [--dense-suffix-array | --no-dense-suffix-array]\n                          [--platform {illumina,dnbseq,ultima,pacbio,hifi,clr,ont,nanopore}]\n                          [--sample SAMPLE] [--format {BAM,CRAM}]\n                          [--output-profile {default,archive,calling}] [--chunk-size CHUNK_SIZE]\n                          [--force] [--force-step STEP] [--dry-run] [--help-all]\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --r1 R1               Read 1 FASTQ file\n  --r2 R2               Read 2 FASTQ file (optional)\n  --aligner {auto,bwa,bwa-mem2,bwa-mem3,minimap2,pbmm2}\n                        Aligner to use (matches the 'align' command).\n  --fast                Align short reads with bwa-mem3's --fast speed preset (implies --aligner\n                        bwa-mem3), about twice as fast. Its alignments differ from BWA's\n                        (docs/hardware.md has a measurement); the run warns (bwa_mem3_fast).\n                        Without it bwa-mem3 writes BWA's exact records.\n  --prefer-native-gpu, --no-prefer-native-gpu\n                        Prefer native GPU alignment. Windows CUDA is automatic when it fits; --no-\n                        prefer-native-gpu forces CPU there.\n  --dense-suffix-array, --no-dense-suffix-array\n                        Have wgse-bwa (0.11.0 or later) read the reference's dense suffix-array\n                        file, built by `ref dense-sa`: the same alignments, faster, holding the\n                        file in RAM (0.8 GB for hs38). Used only when wgse-bwa aligns: otherwise\n                        (another aligner, such as wgse-bwa-mem3 standing in, no file, or no such\n                        wgse-bwa) the run warns (dense_suffix_array_unused) and aligns without it.\n                        Default: config dense_suffix_array, else off.\n  --platform {illumina,dnbseq,ultima,pacbio,hifi,clr,ont,nanopore}\n                        Sequencing platform (default: detected from read groups or read names,\n                        else illumina).\n  --sample SAMPLE       Sample name to embed in read-group metadata.\n  --format {BAM,CRAM}   Output format (default: BAM)\n  --output-profile {default,archive,calling}\n                        Lossless output: default keeps --format; archive selects CRAM 3.1 (GATK\n                        >=4.7); calling selects CRAM 3.0. Default: config output_profile, else\n                        default. See docs/ont.md.\n  --chunk-size CHUNK_SIZE\n                        ONT/PacBio input per chunk (K/M/G/T; 0: one pass). Resumes unfinished\n                        chunks and bounds temporary files. Requires sorted, indexed input; ignored\n                        with --keep-fastq.\n  --help-all            Show this help with the advanced options too, and exit.\n\nglobal options:\n  -i/--input, -o/--outdir, --genome, --ref, -t/--threads, -m/--memory, --json, --quiet, --debug,\n  --config and the other global options are accepted before or after the command; 'wgsextract\n  --help' lists them all.\n\nresuming:\n  Completed steps are recorded in <output>.steps.json and skipped when a rerun's inputs,\n  settings and tools are unchanged.\n\n  --force, --no-resume  Rerun every step, ignoring the steps an earlier run completed.\n  --force-step STEP     Rerun STEP and the steps that use its outputs (repeatable). Steps:\n                        extract, align, index.\n  --dry-run             Print which steps would run or be skipped, and why, then exit.\n\n9 advanced options not shown; 'wgsextract realign --help-all' lists them.\n\nexamples:\n  wgsextract realign --input sample_hg19.bam --ref hs38.fa\n  wgsextract realign --input sample_hg19.bam --ref hs38.fa --dry-run\n\noutputs (in the output directory unless noted):\n  <input stem>_aligned.bam or .cram (+ index); with --keep-fastq, also <input\n  stem>_R1/_R2.fastq.gz (single-end ONT/PacBio: <input stem>.fastq.gz).\n  Completed steps are recorded in <output>.steps.json, so a rerun resumes\n  after a crash.\n\nneeds:\n  samtools; depending on options and inputs, also: bcftools, bgzip, bwa, bwa-\n  mem2, bwa-mem3, curl, gatk, minimap2, pbmm2, sambamba, samblaster; hours on\n  a 30x genome.\n",
